        "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16}=1.$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, there are no $y$-intercepts.",
